# Client routes — SCOPED, deliberately. No wildcard `/* / 200`: a
# wildcard makes Cloudflare Pages answer every missing hashed asset
# with index.html as 200 text/html, which strict-MIME refuses and the
# immutable cache header then pins for a year. Adding a route in
# src/Route.elm means adding a line here by name, and that friction is
# the point. CI fails the build if a wildcard appears.
#
# The target is `/`, NEVER `/index.html`. Pages canonicalises every
# `/index.html` address to `/` with a 308 and applies that to a
# rewrite's target too, so `/about /index.html 200` bounced a shared
# link to the front page; and its parser rejects a splat whose target
# ends in `/index.html` as an infinite loop and silently drops the
# rule, so `/recipe/* /index.html 200` was never in force at all and
# every recipe link landed on the 404. Both held from the first deploy
# until 2026-10-04. `scripts/redirects_test.ts` holds the form.

/about / 200
/design-standard / 200
/list / 200
/plan / 200
/restaurant-roulette / 200

# The one wildcard, and the one route that cannot be named: a recipe's
# slug is CONTENT, and adding a recipe must not mean editing this file.
#
# Scoped to /recipe/, which is what makes it safe where a bare `/*` is
# not. The failure `/*` causes is that a missing hashed asset comes
# back as index.html with a 200 and a text/html type; hashed assets
# live under /assets/ and nothing but a recipe address can ever fall
# into /recipe/. CI still rejects a root-level wildcard.
/recipe/* / 200
